ere's a very particular political  divide on the concept of American Empire  and a lot of that divide splits pretty  cleanly depending on where one sort of  is on the political spectrum in thinking  back to the the guano era so to speak  and the time where we were seeing  westward expansion within the what is  now the contiguous 48 states in the 19th  century the  expansion was starting into the  Caribbean in Pacific Islands was there  an equivalent political split amongst  the population in terms of who supported  this island expansion and who did not or  were political circumstances different  such that that debate wasn't really  taking place no there really was a  debate throughout the 19th century as  the United States as borders expanded  westward even before it started to  expand overseas expansion was  contentious and generally the two poles  of contention not the only ones but the  main ones were between expansionist who  wanted to see the borders of the United  States grow and who wanted to claim new  lands for the for the country and  racists who were concerned about  incorporating too many people who were  perceived as non-white into the Union so  it's an interesting moment when the  United States expands quite a lot over  the course of the 19th century but  actually doesn't incorporate a large  number of people as a percentage of its  own population and a lot of the borders  of the United States when it's expanding  in the 19th century are carefully  curated so the United States can claim  for example as much of Mexico as it can  get while still claiming as few Mexicans  as it can and this comes up in the  debate about the guano Islands one thing  that gets that guano Islands act through  Congress is the proviso that any guano  islands that are claims the United  States has to be uninhabited so this can  be land that can be added to the United  States and new locations that will be  part of the United States but they won't  bring new peoples into the Union so the  idea of the peoples into the Union is  interesting because one of the things  that I I would argue and you might agree  you might disagree has sort of changed  in the conversation about American  Empire since the 1800s that that you and  I have been talking about so far is that  I think today one of the most sort of  important elements of American Empire is  socio-cultural empire the idea of music  movies television literature when I go  and visit I was born in Argentina when I  go visit my family in Argentina there's  an awareness of that  Bread of American Empire that is not  reciprocal in the United States from  most other countries what is your sense  of the importance of that today is it a  form of empire and when did this sort of  start to shift would you say yes oh  there's a debate now about whether it's  proper to characterize the United States  as an empire and there are a lot of  aspects that you could note if you  wanted to make that argument one is the  extent of US culture and the way that  that's non-reciprocal that if you're in  Argentina you end up knowing a lot more  about US music and if you're in the  United States and you're thinking about  Argentinian music it's also in terms of  the USS military involvement in dozens  of countries you know at any one time  around the world the polite term for  this or the positive term for this would  be influence leadership a critical or  negative term often used as a pejorative  would be Empire margin is a little  different it's not about whether the  United States is behaving badly is  imposing itself on other countries I  just followed the territory and I just  looked at the territorial shape of the  United States and make a fairly obvious  you know observation that the United  States has had territories and  particularly overseas territories for  the bulk of its history so in that sort  of analytical sense it's it's just you  know on the map the United States is an  empire and I think it's that